12_23_23_27_53	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Chess player. Chess is a 2-player game playing on a 8x8 board, and the goal is to checkmate your opponent\'s king.\n        ## Pieces & Movement:\n\n        * King (K): Moves one square in any direction.\n\n        * Queen (Q): Moves any distance in any straight or diagonal line.\n\n        * Rook (R): Moves any distance horizontally or vertically.\n\n        * Bishop (B): Moves any distance diagonally.\n\n        * Knight (N): Moves in "L" shape: 2 squares one way + 1 square perpendicular.\n\n        * Pawn (P):\n\n            * Forward 1 square (never backward)\n\n            * First move: optionally 2 squares forward\n\n            * Captures diagonally forward\n\n            * Promotion: Reaches last rank → any piece (except king)\n        \n        ## Special rules: \n        * Castling: King moves 2 squares toward rook, rook jumps over. Conditions:\n\n            * Neither piece has moved\n\n            * No pieces between\n\n            * King not in check\n\n            * King doesn\'t pass through attacked squares\n\n        * En Passant: Pawn that moved 2 squares can be captured by adjacent enemy pawn as if it moved 1 square.\n\n        * Check: King under attack must escape immediately.\n\n        * Checkmate: King in check with no legal moves → game ends.\n\n        * Stalemate: King not in check but has no legal moves → draw.\n\n        ## Board and Chess piece Representation:\n        * Board is represented as [a-h][1-8]\n        * Origin: bottom-left corner (a1) \n        * White side: Bottom (ranks 1-2)\n        * Black side: Top (ranks 7-8)\n        * X-axis: Files `a` to `h` (left to right)\n        * Y-axis: Ranks `1` to `8` (bottom to top)\n\n        ## Piece encoding format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n        \n        * Type (Piece Type):\n            * `K` - King\n            * `Q`- Queen\n            * `R`- Rook\n            * `B` - Bishop\n            * `K` - Knight\n            * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|NBF0|BBF0|QBF0|KBF0|BBF0|NBF0|RBF0| 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0|PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|NWF0|RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, P: e2-e4, P: e2-e3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, N: g1-f3, N: g1-h3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': "Okay, let's see. I'm playing as White in a standard chess position. The board is set up with all the pieces in their starting positions. My valid moves are all the possible first moves for White. Since it's the beginning of the game, all pawns can move either one or two squares forward, and the knights can move to their usual starting squares.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the standard opening principles. In chess, the main goals in the opening are to control the center, develop pieces (especially knights and bishops), and ensure king safety (usually by castling). Since it's the very first move, I can't castle yet, but I can start working on the center.\n\nLooking at the valid moves, the pawn moves to the center (d2-d4, e2-e4) are usually the strongest because they immediately stake a claim on the central squares. The other pawn moves (a, b, c, f, g, h files) are less common as first moves because they don't control the center as effectively. The knight moves (N: b1-a3, b1-c3, g1-f3, g1-h3) are also possible, but developing knights early is good, but usually after moving pawns to control the center.\n\nThe most common first moves in chess are e2-e4 and d2-d4. e4 is the King's Pawn Opening, which is very aggressive and aims to control d5 and f5. d4 is the Queen's Pawn Opening, controlling c5 and e5. Both are solid choices.\n\nNow, considering the valid moves list, e2-e4 and d2-d4 are both present. Let me check the list again. Yes, P: e2-e4 and P: d2-d4 are options. Also, e2-e3 and d2-d3 are there, but those are less common as they don't immediately contest the center as strongly.\n\nThe knight moves like N: g1-f3 are also good because they develop the knight to a strong square, attacking the center. But since it's the first move, moving a pawn is generally preferred to open lines for the bishops and queen.\n\nBetween e4 and d4, e4 is slightly more common in practice because it opens more lines for the queen and the light-squared bishop. However, d4 is also very strong. But as a beginner or intermediate player, e4 is often recommended as the first move.\n\nWait, but in the valid moves list, the pawn moves for e2 are e2-e4 and e2-e3. Similarly for d2. So the best options are e4 or d4.\n\nAnother thing to consider is that moving the e-pawn to e4 allows the bishop on f1 to develop more easily. Also, e4 leads to more open games which are easier for beginners to handle.\n\nI should also check if there's any immediate threat, but in the starting position, there are none. So it's just about optimal opening play.\n\nThe other pawn moves like c2-c4 (English Opening) are also possible but less common as the first move. Similarly, f2-f4 (Bird's Opening) is a bit more unusual and can weaken the king's position.\n\nSo, between e4 and d4, which is better? In terms of statistics, e4 has a slightly higher winning percentage for White, but both are excellent choices. However, since the problem says to maximize the probability of winning, choosing the most aggressive and central move makes sense.\n\nTherefore, the best move here is P: e2-e4.", 'output': 'Situation: Standard starting position with all pieces in initial setup. As White, I have the first move advantage and must choose an opening move to gain central control and develop pieces efficiently. The board is symmetric, no immediate threats, and all standard opening principles apply.\n\nEvaluation: Key options are central pawn pushes (e2-e4, d2-d4) or knight development (g1-f3).  \n- **e2-e4**: Maximizes central control (d5/f5 squares), opens diagonals for queen/bishop, and is statistically the strongest first move (highest win rate for White).  \n- **d2-d4**: Strong alternative for central control but slightly less dynamic for immediate piece activity.  \n- **g1-f3**: Solid knight development but delays central pawn commitment; better after e/d-pawn moves.  \n- **Other pawn moves** (e.g., c2-c4, f2-f4): Less optimal as first moves—they either neglect the center (a/b/c files) or weaken king safety (f-file).  \n\nReason: e2-e4 is the most aggressive and principled choice. It seizes the center, accelerates development, and aligns with historical and statistical best practices for maximizing winning chances. While d2-d4 is also strong, e2-e4 creates more immediate tactical opportunities and is harder for Black to counter symmetrically.\n\nAnswer: P: e2-e4'}
