ey haven't moved.\n\nWait, the board's row 1 is the bottom (White's side), row 8 is top (Black's side). So:\n\nRow 1 (bottom): a1 is Rook (RWF0), g1 is King (KWF1). So White's king is on g1, which is unusual because normally it's on e1. So maybe castling happened or the king moved.\n\nRow 2: pawns at b2, c2, f2, g2 (all PWF0, so they haven't moved yet). Wait, but a3 has a pawn (PWF1), which is on rank 3, so that pawn moved from a2 to a3.\n\nBlack's king is on c8 (KBF1). So Black's king is on c8, which is not the usual e8. Maybe the game is in an endgame scenario.\n\nNow, the valid moves for White are:\n\nR: a1-a2, R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, R: a1-e1, R: a1-f1,\n\nP: a3-a4,\n\nP: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3,\n\nN: d5-b4, d5-b6, d5-c3, d5-c7, d5-e3, d5-e7, d5-f4, d5-f6,\n\nK: g1-f1, g1-h1, g1-h2.\n\nFirst, I need to assess the position. White's king is on g1 (KWF1), which has moved (since Moved is 1). So castling is not possible for the king anymore. The rook on a1 is RWF0 (hasn't moved), but since the king has moved, castling is not an option regardless.\n\nBlack's king is on c8. The White knight is on d5. Let's see possible threats.\n\nWhat's the immediate concern? Is the Black king in check? Let's check if any White pieces are attacking c8.\n\nThe knight on d5: from d5, a knight moves in L-shapes. To reach c8, it would need to go from d5 to, say, c7 (which is two up and one left: d5 to c7 is one left, two up? Wait, d5 to c7 is (d to c is left one, 5 to 7 is up two), yes, that's a knight move. So N on d5 can move to c7, which is adjacent to Black king on c8. Wait, c7 is one square below c8. So if the knight moves to c7, it would be attacking the Black king? Wait, c8 is the king's position. From c7, the knight would be two squares away from c8 (knight moves from c7 to, say, b5, etc.), no. Wait, knight on c7 would attack squares like b5, d5, a6, e6, etc. Wait, no: from c7, knight moves are:\n\nc7 to a6 (left 2, down 1