hich is not on rank 6, so not attacked by rook on e6.\n\nSimilarly, moving to c5 (down one), which is rank 5.\n\nSo again, not checkmate.\n\nBut moving to e6 may be better than c5 because... Well, let's compare.\n\nIf rook moves to c5 (same file as king's file), then rook is on c5, attacking up the c-file. King is on c6, so rook on c5 checks vertically. Then king can move to b5, b6, b7, c7, d5, d6, d7.\n\nBut rook on c5 would control the c-file, so king can't stay on c-file. So king must leave c-file.\n\nSimilarly, with rook on e6, controlling rank 6.\n\nBut which move gives more pressure?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rook can move to c5, which is closer to the king, but also, are there any pawns that can support?\n\nWait, White has a pawn on a3. If the rook moves to c5, perhaps supporting a pawn advance?\n\nAlternatively, let's look at the pawn moves. For example, pushing pawns to advance. But the Black king is on c6, which is in the center. Maybe White wants to activate the king or pawns.\n\nWait, the White king is on g1, which is the corner. It's not very active. But the valid moves for the king are limited: f1, h1, h2. Not very useful.\n\nLooking at pawn moves: advancing pawns. For example, b2-b4 or b2-b3. But why? The Black king is on c6, so maybe White wants to create a passed pawn or something. But with the rook active, maybe the best move is to check the king and force it to move, possibly into a worse position.\n\nBetween rook moves to c5 and e6, which is better?\n\nLet's consider e5-c5: rook moves left to c5, checking the king on c6. Black king must move. Suppose king moves to b5. Then White could follow up with rook taking control of the file or maybe king advancing.\n\nAlternatively, rook to e6: checks on the rank. If king moves to d6, but wait, rook is on e6, so d6 is adjacent? No, d6 is one left from e6. So king on c6 moving to d6 would be towards the rook. Wait, king on c6 to d6 is possible, but then rook on e6 would still attack d6? No, because rook o