f the rook moves to e6, then it's on the 6th rank, same as the king's rank. Then, e6 would be attacking along the 6th rank towards g6. Wait, e6 to g6 is two squares to the right. So if the rook moves to e6, then it's on the same rank as the black king (6th), but separated by two files (e to f to g). So the rook at e6 would be attacking the squares along the 6th rank, so yes, it would be checking the king at g6. Because from e6, moving right along the rank, it can reach g6.\n\nWait, yes. So if White moves Rook from e5 to e6, then the rook is on e6, which is same rank as black king at g6 (rank 6). So the rook would be attacking along the 6th rank, so the black king is in check. Because e6 to g6 is two squares east, so the rook's line of attack would include g6.\n\nWait, but in the current position, is the black king in check? No, because the rook is at e5. So e5 to g6 is not a straight line. e5 is rank 5, g6 is rank 6. So diagonal? No, rook moves straight. So no, currently not in check.\n\nBut if White plays Re6, then the rook is on e6, same rank as black king (rank 6), so yes, that would put the black king in check.\n\nWait, but looking at the valid moves, R: e5-e6 is one of the options. So that's a possible move.\n\nWait, but let's check: the rook at e5 moving to e6. Then, the rook would be on e6, which is directly on the same rank (6) as the black king at g6. So the rook would be attacking along the 6th rank towards the king. So yes, that's a check.\n\nBut is there any obstruction? Between e6 and g6 is f6. Is there any piece there? Looking at the board: row 6 has only KBF1 at g6, others are empty. So f6 is empty. So Re6 would attack g6, putting black king in check.\n\nSo Re6 would be a check. That seems strong.\n\nAlternatively, moving the rook to g5 (R: e5-g5), which is same file as black king's file (g), but rank 5. Then, from g5, moving up to g6 would be possible. So if rook is at g5, then it's on the same file as the black king (g6), so that would check the kin