\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a3-a4, P: b4-b5, K: f3-e2, K: f3-e3, K: f3-e4, K: f3-f4, K: f3-g2, K: f3-g3, K: f3-g4, P: g5-g6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': "Okay, let's see. I'm playing as White here. The board setup is a bit sparse. Let me parse the current state.\n\nFirst, looking at the board representation. The rows are numbered from 8 at the top to 1 at the bottom. Files a to h left to right. White's side is bottom (ranks 1-2), Black's top (7-8).\n\nCurrent pieces:\n\n- Black King is at c7 (KBF1). So it's Black's King, moved before (since Moved=1), and 2Step=F (so not en passant eligible here, but kings don't do en passant anyway).\n\nWhite pieces:\n\n- At f3: KWF1. Wait, that's a White King? Wait, K is King, W is color, F is 2Step (F for no), 1 is moved. So yes, White King is at f3. Wait, but kings are denoted as K. So KWF1 is White King, has moved (since Moved=1), so castling is not possible anymore. But where are the other pieces?\n\nOther White pieces:\n\n- a3: PWF1 (White Pawn, moved, not en passant)\n- b4: PWF1\n- g5: PWF1\n- f2: PWF0 (White Pawn at f2, never moved before, so can move 2 squares first move)\n\nWait, looking at the board:\n\nRow 7 (Black's side): c7 has KBF1 (Black King)\nRow 5: g5 has PWF1 (White pawn)\nRow 4: b4 has PWF1\nRow 3: a3 has PWF1, f3 has KWF1 (White King)\nRow 2: f2 has PWF0 (White pawn that hasn't moved yet)\n\nWait, the board:\n\nRow 8: empty\nRow 7: c7 is Black King\nRow 6: empty\nRow 5: g5 is White pawn\nRow 4: b4 is White pawn\nRow 3: a3 (White pawn), f3 (White King)\nRow 2: f2 (White pawn, hasn't moved)\nRow 1: empty\n\nWait, but pawns move forward. Since White is on bottom (ranks 1-2), their pawns move up (increasing rank). So for White, pawns move from rank 2 to 3, etc.\n\nWait, the White King is at f3. That's quite advanced. Normally kings stay back, but here it's on f3. Maybe this is an endgame scenario.\n\nBlack's King is at c7. So both kings are somewhat in the center but on opposite sides.\n\nThe other pawns: White has pawns at a3, b4, g5, and f2 (which is at starting position). Black has only the king? Wait, the board shows only Black's king on c7. Are there other Black pieces? The problem states the current state representation, so if other squares are empty, then yes. So Black only has a king, White has king and four pawns.\n\nWait, but that seems odd. Maybe I misread. Let me check again.\n\nLooking at the board description:\n\nRow 8: all empty.\n\nRow 7: c7 has KBF1 (Black King)\n\nRow 6: empty.\n\nRow 5: g5 has PWF1 (White pawn)\n\nRow 4: b4 has PWF1\n\nRow 3: a3 (PWF1), f3 (KWF1)\n\nRow 2: f2 (PWF0)\n\nRow 1: empty.\n\nSo White has King at f3, pawns at a3, b4, g5, f2. Black has only King at c7.\n\nWait, but in chess, you can't have a position with only kings unless it's a stalemate or something, but here White has pawns. So this must be a very endgame position where Black has lost all pieces except king, and White has some pawns.\n\nThe goal for White is to checkmate Black's king. But with only pawns and king, how?\n\nBut first, let's see the valid moves given:\n\nValid moves are:\n\nP: a3-a4,\n\nP: b4-b5,\n\nK: f3-e2,\n\nK: f3-e3,\n\nK: f3-e4,\n\nK: f3-f4,\n\nK: f3-g2,\n\nK: f3-g3,\n\nK: f3-g4,\n\nP: g5-g6\n\nSo White's possible moves are moving the king or advancing pawns.\n\nWait, the pawns:\n\na3 can move to a4 (since it's a pawn moving forward; as White, moving up the board, so rank 3 to 4 is forward)\n\nb4 to b5\n\ng5 to g6\n\nf2's pawn is at starting position (PWF0), but its valid moves aren't