-pawn is on g5, so two squares away from promotion (g6, g7, g8). Wait, g5 to g6 is one move, then g7 is another, then g8. So two more moves after g6 to promote.\n\nBut Black's king is at c7, which is quite far. It would take several moves for Black's king to reach the g-file.\n\nLet's calculate:\n\nFrom c7 to g7 is 4 files over (c->d->e->f->g), so 4 moves. But Black king can move one square per turn. So to get to g7, needs at least 4 moves.\n\nWhite's g-pawn is at g5. After moving to g6 (this turn), then g7 (next), then g8 (promote). So three moves total. Wait, no: g5 to g6 (1), g6 to g7 (2), g7 to g8 (3). So three moves to promote.\n\nBut Black's king would take 4 moves to reach g7. So if White pushes the pawn three times, it promotes before Black king can stop it.\n\nWait, but during those moves, White has to make three pawn moves, but Black gets to move each time.\n\nWait, no: each side alternates. So if White moves g5-g6 (move 1), Black can move king towards it. Then White moves g6-g7 (move 2), Black moves again. Then White g7-g8=Q.\n\nSo let's see:\n\nCurrent position: White to move.\n\nIf White plays g5-g6:\n\nBlack's king can start moving towards, say, d6 (c7 to d6 is one move), then e5, etc.\n\nBut let's see:\n\nAfter 1. g6, Black's king can go to c6 (closer), then next White moves g7, Black to c5, White g8=Q. Then Black king is still far, but White can use king to support.\n\nAlternatively, maybe pushing the g-pawn is the fastest way.\n\nAlternatively, the a and b pawns are on the queenside. But Black's king is also on the queenside (c7), so it's closer to stop those.\n\nFor example, a3-a4: Black king can move to b6, then capture the pawn if it advances.\n\nSimilarly, b4-b5: Black king can go to c5 or b6.\n\nSo perhaps the g-pawn is the best candidate for promotion since it's on the opposite side from Black's king.\n\nThus, moving g5-g6 seems good.\n\nBut let's check the valid moves: P: g5-g6 is an option.\n\nOther options: moving the king. Why would White want to move the king?\n\nThe White king is at f3. Maybe to support the pawns. For example, moving towards the center or towards the pawns.\n\nBut in endgames, king activity is important. The king should support the pawns.\n\nCurrently, the White king is at f3. If the g-pawn is pushed, the king might want to move towards e4 or f4 to support it.\n\nBut let's see possible king moves:\n\nOptions include f3-e4, f3-g4, etc.\n\nBut if White moves the king first, maybe loses a tempo. Whereas pushing the pawn immediately starts the promotion process.\n\nBut let's consider if moving the king could be better.\n\nFor example, if White moves Kf3-e4, putting the king in a more central position. Then next move support the g-pawn.\n\nBut the question is, which move gives the highest chance to promote.\n\nAnother thing to consider: the Black king is at c7. If White can create a passed pawn and push it, while keeping the Black king away.\n\nThe g-pawn is currently on g5. If pushed to g6, it's one step closer. Black king is far away.\n\nAlternatively, if White moves the king to g4, it could support the pawn further.\n\nWait, but the immediate pawn push seems critical.\n\nWait, but let's check if g5-g6 is safe. After g5-g6, is there any chance Black king can capture it?\n\nAfter g6, the pawn is at g6. Black king is at c7. To capture it, Black king would need to get to f6 or h6, but that's many moves away. So no immediate danger.\n\nThus, pushing g-pawn is good.\n\nBut let's see the other pawn moves. a3-a4: maybe creating a passed pawn on the queenside, but Black king is close. Similarly for b4-b5.\n\nFor example, after a3-a4, Black king moves Kb6, then White a5, Kc5, etc. It might lead to Black capturing the pawn.\n\nWhereas the g-pawn is on the other side, harder for Black king to reach.\n\nSo promoting the g-pawn seems the best plan.\n\nTherefore, moving P: g5-g6 is a strong candidate.\n\nNow, what about king moves? For example, moving the king towards the center or towards the g-pawn.\n\nKf3-g4 would bring the king closer to support the g-pawn. But using a move to do that instead of advancing the pawn might cost a tempo.\n\nBut in some endgames, king support is necessary. However, in this case, the pawn is so close to promotion and Black king is far, maybe the pawn can queen without king support.\n\nLet's simulate:\n\n1. g5-g6 (White)\n\nBlack's move: Kc7-d6 (moving towards the pawn)\n\n2. g6-g7 (White)\n\nBlack: Kd6-e7 (trying to reach g7)\n\n3. g7-g8=Q (promote to queen)\n\nNow White has a queen, which can easily checkmate the Black king.\n\nSo this line works.\n\nAlternatively, if Black tries to move towards the pawn faster:\n\nAfter 1. g6, Black plays Kc7-c6\n\n2. g7, Kc6-d5\n\n3. g8=Q. Now White can check with Qg5+ or similar, but Black king is still far.\n\nSo promotion happens in three moves, which Black can't stop.\n\nTherefore, pushing the g-pawn is the way to go.\n\nNow, looking at the valid moves, P: g5-g6 is an option.\n\nOther pawn moves: a3-a4 and b4-b5. But as mentioned, those are on the queenside where Black king is located.\n\nFor example, if White plays a3-a4:\n\nBlack king can go Kb6, then if White a5, Kc5, and Black can capture the pawn if it advances further.\n\nSimilarly, b4-b5: Black king Kc5, then White b6, Kb4, etc. Black might capture.\n\nSo those are riskier.\n\nKing moves: moving the king could be useful for supporting pawns, but in this case, the g-pawn can promote without immediate support.\n\nHowever, after promoting, White will need to checkmate with king and queen versus lone king, which is straightforward. But during the promotion process, the king might need to be positioned correctly.\n\nBut the immediate push of the pawn seems best.\n\nAnother consideration: the White king is currently at f3. If it stays there, after promoting to queen, the king might be a bit far, but the queen can handle the checkmate.\n\nAlternatively, moving the king up could help, but again, the pawn push is more urgent.\n\nSo the best move here is P: g5-g6.\n\nWait, but let's check if there's any stalemate risk or other issues.\n\nBlack's only piece is the king, so as long as White doesn't stalemate, it's fine. But with pawns moving, it's unlikely.\n\nAnother angle: en passant. But none of the pawns here are moving two squares except possibly the f2 pawn, but it's blocked. So en passant isn't a factor here.\n\nAlso, castling isn't possible since the king has moved (KWF1 indicates Moved=1).\n\nSo yes, g5-g6 seems the strongest move.\n\nNow, looking at the valid moves list, P: g5-g6 is present.\n\nSo the answer should be P: g5-g6.\n\nWait, but let's check if any other move could be better.\n\nFor example, moving the king to e4. What does that do?\n\nKf3-e4: centralizes the king. Maybe prepares to support both central and kingside pawns.\n\nBut the g-pawn is the most advanced. So using a move to push the pawn is better than king move.\n\nAnother thought: if White moves the king towards the Black king, maybe to restrict it. But Black king is far away.\n\nAlternatively, the White king could march towards the queenside to support the a and b pawns, but again, the g-pawn is closer to promotion.\n\nSo no, pushing g-pawn is better.\n\nThus, the best move is P: g5-g6.", 'output': "Situation: White has a king at f3 and pawns at a3, b4, g5, f2 (blocked by king), while Black's only piece is a king at c7. White's goal is to promote a pawn to deliver checkmate. The g5-pawn is two squares from promotion (g8), and Black's king is far away on the queenside (c7), requiring 4+ moves to reach the g-file. Advancing the g-pawn immediately accelerates promotion, while queenside pawn pushes (a3-a4, b4-b5) risk Black king interception.\n\nEvaluation: \n- **P: g5-g6** directly advances the most promising pawn toward promotion. Black’s king cannot reach the g-file in time to stop promotion (e.g., 1...Kd6 2.g7 Ke7 3.g8=Q). \n- **King moves** (e.g., f3-e4) waste tempo; the king is already positioned to support kingside play but pawn advancement is more urgent. \n- **P: a3-a4 / b4-b5** invite Black king counterplay (e.g., 1.a4 Kb6) and risk pawn loss due to proximity to c7.\n\nReason: Promoting the g-pawn is the fastest path to a decisive material advantage. Delaying with king moves or queenside pushes gives Black time to contest the position. g5-g6 exploits the Black king’s distance and secures a winning promotion sequence.\n\nAnswer: P: g5-g6"}
